WIP

Briefly summarize the requirements and goals of the app you developed. What user needs was this app designed to address?

The app was designed to help users manage inventory by providing a secure login, a database for items and users, and the ability to add, remove, and update item quantities. It also included notifications when stock reached zero, addressing the need for efficient and reliable inventory tracking.

What screens and features were necessary to support user needs and produce a user-centered UI for the app? How did your UI designs keep users in mind? Why were your designs successful?

The app required three main screens: login, inventory grid, and notification permissions. The UI kept users in mind by using clear layouts, grid-based displays, and simple icons for actions. This made it easy to read and interact with, supporting quick decisions and efficient management.

How did you approach the process of coding your app? What techniques or strategies did you use? How could those techniques or strategies be applied in the future?

I broke the project into smaller modules, starting with the database and login system, then moving to inventory functionality and notifications. I used modular coding and incremental testing. These strategies can be applied to future projects to keep code organized and manageable.

I tested the app using an emulator, checking edge cases like incorrect logins. Testing was important to confirm functionality and revealed small usability issues that could be fixed early.


How did you test to ensure your code was functional? Why is this process important, and what did it reveal?
Consider the full app design and development process from initial planning to finalization. Where did you have to innovate to overcome a challenge?

A major challenge was providing full functionality without cluttering the interface. I overcame this by using a grid layout with plus/minus icons instead of text-heavy options, keeping the UI simple but powerful.


In what specific component of your mobile app were you particularly successful in demonstrating your knowledge, skills, and experience?

I was especially successful in the inventory grid system, which demonstrated my ability to connect UI design with back-end logic while keeping the user experience clear and efficient.
